# 1230 — 2024-12-30, uclibc 9.1.0

## Release

    build_time     20241230152545   (libraries)
    project_commit 8e24a5b
    sdk_commit     9e4d64f
    mhal_commit    da23dd9

Read the stamp out of any file with:

    strings libmi_sys.so | grep 'Sigmastar Module'

## Origin

Harvested from the camera's own shipped firmware, not from a vendor release
tree -- weaker provenance than 0907 beside it, and the same standing as the
infinity6e entry. The board is a no-name SSC377QE with an imx335. Its flash
carries two squashfs rootfs images; this is the second, from

    /config/lib/

Note that the same image also carries a *different* MI set at `/lib`, which is
what `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` in the vendor's `script/demo.sh` points at. That one is a
2024-06 build carrying the same commit triple as OpenIPC's. It is **not** the
set that matches the kernel modules and it is not what is here. Pair the two
halves by commit triple, never by what the vendor's own start script puts on the
library path:

    /config/lib   project 8e24a5b  sdk 9e4d64f  mhal da23dd9   <- carried here
    /lib          2024-06, OpenIPC's triple                    <- not carried

## uclibc only

The camera ships one C library flavour and this is it. There is no glibc variant
to carry, unlike 0907. Every MI library here is `NEEDED libc.so.0`.

That is the reason this drop exists in the tree at all: the port was originally
built against glibc and needed a substituted CamOs wrapper, cleared execstack
and an `LD_PRELOAD` to get as far as failing. On a uClibc rootfs the whole set
is a straight drop-in with none of that.

## Three libraries are glibc-linked anyway

    libADAS_LINUX.so   libg711.so   libg726.so

carry `NEEDED libc.so.6` in an otherwise uClibc set. This is the vendor's own
inconsistency, not a packaging error here -- 0907's uclibc `libg711`/`libg726`
are properly uClibc-linked, so these three regressed between drops.

They will not load on a uClibc rootfs without a `libc.so.6` symlink pointing at
libuClibc, which thingino does not create. None of the three is in the Raptor
HAL's dlopen set, so nothing currently misses them. They are carried rather than
dropped because this is an archive of what the vendor shipped.

## Two libraries are GNU-hash-only

    libcam_os_wrapper.so   libcam_fs_wrapper.so

have a `.gnu.hash` section and no SysV `.hash`. A uClibc-ng ldso built without
`LDSO_GNU_HASH_SUPPORT` cannot resolve anything in them, and reports the failure
as `can't resolve symbol` with a NULL `dlerror()`. libcam_os_wrapper is the
first library the HAL dlopens, so the whole stack fails at init. thingino
enables that option via `configs/fragments/uclibc/gnu-hash.config`.

Static symbol closure does not detect this: it reports the symbols as present,
because they are.

## The cam wrappers are a later rebuild

    libcam_os_wrapper.so   build_time.20250601102605   wrapper.v2.0.43
    libcam_fs_wrapper.so   build_time.20250601102607   wrapper.v0.0.8

Six months after the MI libraries, with an empty `kernel_commit`. The wrapper
*versions* are identical to 0907's, so this is a rebuild of the same source
rather than a newer wrapper. Expected in a harvested image: the vendor rebuilt
the firmware after the SDK drop was cut.

## Relationship to 0907

A superset by name. All 48 of 0907/uclibc's filenames are present, and it adds

    libADAS_LINUX.so   libAGC_LINUX.so   libANR_LINUX.so

None of the 48 shared names is byte-identical to its 0907 counterpart -- this is
a whole generation apart, not a respin.

Smaller despite carrying three more libraries: 3.4M against 0907/uclibc's 5.6M.

## Hardware

Proven on the SSC377QE above, on a thingino uClibc rootfs, encoding 1080p H.264
at 25fps with no shims of any kind. Single stream. A second encoder channel
still fails to bind (`MI_SYS_BindChnPort2` -> `0xA0092008`), identically on this
drop and on 0907, so that is not a property of either drop.

