# 0907 — 2022-09-07, glibc 11.1.0 and uclibc 9.1.0

## Release

    release_0907   built 2022-09-07
    project_commit 3a7aec9
    sdk_commit     3dcd67a
    mhal_commit    a7acd5c

Both variants come from the same drop and carry the same three commits. Their
build stamps are minutes apart:

    glibc  11.1.0   build_time.20220907112126
    uclibc  9.1.0   build_time.20220907111141

## Origin

Taken from the vendor SDK release tree, not harvested from a shipped firmware
and not taken from OpenIPC. This is a stronger provenance than the 1230 entry
above and than the infinity6e entry beside it, both of which were reconstructed
from camera images.

    ssc377/release_0907/sourcecode/project/release/chip/i6c/ipc/common/
      glibc/11.1.0/mi_libs/dynamic/
      uclibc/9.1.0/mi_libs/dynamic/

OpenIPC's sigmastar-osdrv-infinity6c ships the same drop -- identical
project/sdk/mhal commits -- but only the uclibc 9.1.0 variant, and 20 of its
31 libraries. It is a subset of what is here, so nothing was taken from it.

## Why this drop and not the other two

The SSC377 material carries three releases, all on kernel 5.10.61 and all with
the same 32 glibc / 31 uclibc library names. They differ in what built them:

    release_0602   glibc  cb0a0d4/50f6653/20679ab  built 2022-05-11
                   uclibc b5d87b9/5edf260/4830206  built 2022-06-02
    release_0712   both   d191740/e2d9178/371ffee  built 2022-07-12
    release_0907   both   3a7aec9/3dcd67a/a7acd5c  built 2022-09-07

release_0602 is not internally consistent: its two C library variants were
built from different commits three weeks apart, so choosing a variant there
changes the code and not merely the codegen. release_0712's own release note
(Maruko_ILS00V1.0.0) calls it a Beta release. release_0907 is the newest, its
variants agree, and OpenIPC independently shipped this same drop -- a second
party reaching the same choice.

## The two variants are not interchangeable

They differ in content, not only in codegen. glibc carries two libraries uclibc
does not:

    libmi_divp.so
    libsigma3a.so

so the C library component of the path is load-bearing, and a build that picks
the wrong one is missing libraries rather than getting differently compiled
ones.

    glibc  11.1.0   32 libraries
    uclibc  9.1.0   31 libraries

    md5 libmi_sys.so  glibc   f7ff32117b58c0f29f2c0b7533306c78
    md5 libmi_sys.so  uclibc  0c3e8b543918b509ddaa1b1f8b5ee2ac

## sigma_common_libs, merged in

The vendor splits its libraries in two, and this repository does not. The
second set is carried because the Raptor HAL dlopens several of them --
libcam_os_wrapper, libMD_LINUX, libmi_ive -- and it is merged into lib/ rather
than kept beside it:

    ssc377/release_0907/sourcecode/project/release/chip/i6c/
      ipc/common/<libc>/<ver>/mi_libs/dynamic/      32 glibc / 31 uclibc
      sigma_common_libs/<libc>/<ver>/dynamic/       15 glibc / 17 uclibc

Merged because nothing downstream distinguishes them: both sets install to
/usr/lib and the HAL dlopens by name, not by directory. infinity6e beside this
is already flat for the same reason -- it was harvested from a firmware image,
where libcam_os_wrapper and libmi_ive sit among the MI libraries -- so keeping
i6c split would have made it the odd family out and forced a second install
glob in sigmastar-lib.mk. 1230 above is flat for that reason too.

The merge is lossless: no filename appears in both vendor sets, in either C
library variant, so nothing shadows anything. What is lost is the vendor's own
grouping, which is recorded here instead.

Only the dynamic sets are here; the vendor also ships static/, and a
uclibc/4.9.4 variant of the common libraries that neither C library variant in
use here matches.

These carry a different stamp from the MI libraries -- a wrapper version rather
than project/sdk/mhal commits -- and it agrees with the same drop:

    glibc  11.1.0  kernel_commit.14a0a79 build_time.20220907112020 wrapper.v2.0.43
    uclibc  9.1.0  kernel_commit.14a0a79 build_time.20220907111042 wrapper.v2.0.43

    md5 libcam_os_wrapper.so  glibc   ffc25f4392e7126802c16ad5368b72be
    md5 libcam_os_wrapper.so  uclibc  b376e0a5dd2dfbd8f52400f4481018d3

uclibc carries two libraries glibc does not:

    libg711.so
    libg726.so

so the C library component selects content here as well.

The four .txt files are the vendor's own license terms for the AEC and APC
libraries. They are kept beside the binaries they cover rather than dropped,
because this repository redistributes proprietary payload and those are its
terms. 1230 carries the same four.

## Unstamped

    libsigma3a.so

carries no vendor build stamp, so its drop cannot be confirmed from the binary
and is asserted only by the tree it was copied out of. It is glibc-only.

Most of sigma_common_libs is unstamped too -- only the two cam wrappers carry
one. These thirteen are asserted by the tree they came from and nothing else:

    libAEC_LINUX libAED_LINUX libAPC_LINUX libBF_2MIC_LINUX libBF_4MIC_LINUX
    libfbc_decode libMD_LINUX libmi_ive libOD_LINUX libSRC_LINUX
    libSSL_2MIC_LINUX libSSL_4MIC_LINUX libVG_LINUX

The same holds for the corresponding files in 1230.

